**Accepted Evidence**
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Remarks on United States Democracy","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2022-11-02","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/remarks-united-states-democracy","claim":"Biden said democracy was under attack, there is no place for voter intimidation or political violence, and Americans must choose fair and free elections over intimidation and chaos.","locator":"lines 67-79, 84-90, 113-119","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary presidential transcript with direct dated remarks and explicit democratic-norm language.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 target-period rhetoric and democratic framing.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Biden statement as president.","contrary_evidence":["The speech is normative and does not by itself prove institutional reform or long-run change."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.3","4B.8","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Remarks on Voting Rights in Atlanta, Georgia","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2022-01-11","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/remarks-voting-rights-atlanta-georgia","claim":"Biden said he supported changing Senate rules to stop a minority from blocking voting-rights action and framed the choice as democracy over autocracy with the majority should rule in the Senate.","locator":"lines 121-129, 133-136","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary presidential remarks directly stating his voting-rights position and institutional reform preference.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 position on contestability and voting rights.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Biden policy stance and advocacy.","contrary_evidence":["The proposal failed in Congress, so the source shows intent and advocacy more than achieved reform."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.2","4B.7","4B.8","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Statement of Administration Policy: H.R. 8873 - Presidential Election Reform Act","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2022-09-21","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/statement-administration-policy-hr-8873-presidential-election-reform-act","claim":"The administration backed election-count reform to improve the integrity of appointing electors and tallying their votes, preserve the will of the people, and strengthen democracy.","locator":"lines 68-71","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary administration policy statement on a core transfer-of-power reform.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 reform advocacy on transfer rules.","ruler_attribution":"Biden administration position.","contrary_evidence":["This was a policy statement on a bill in Congress, not a signed law or completed institutional reform."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.8","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Justice Department Releases Information on Efforts to Protect the Right to Vote, Prosecute Election Fraud and Secure Elections","publisher":"U.S. Department of Justice","publication_date":"2022-10-26","url":"https://www.justice.gov/archives/opa/pr/justice-department-releases-information-efforts-protect-right-vote-prosecute-election-fraud","claim":"DOJ said it would ensure qualified voters could vote free of discrimination, intimidation, and fraud, monitor compliance on Election Day, and operate complaint channels for voting-rights violations.","locator":"lines 138-180","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary DOJ release with concrete election-protection mechanisms and enforcement roles.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 election-protection operations.","ruler_attribution":"Biden administration DOJ under presidential authority.","contrary_evidence":["The release describes enforcement architecture; it does not prove every local election problem was prevented."],"lenses":["4B.2","4B.4","4B.7","4B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Attorney General Merrick B. Garland Issues New FOIA Guidelines to Favor Disclosure and Transparency","publisher":"U.S. Department of Justice","publication_date":"2022-03-15","url":"https://www.justice.gov/archives/opa/pr/attorney-general-merrick-b-garland-issues-new-foia-guidelines-favor-disclosure-and","claim":"Garland issued FOIA guidelines applying a presumption of openness and emphasizing proactive disclosure as fundamental to open government and accountability.","locator":"lines 139-147","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary DOJ transparency policy with explicit openness language.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 transparency policy.","ruler_attribution":"Biden administration DOJ policy choice.","contrary_evidence":["Transparency guidance does not eliminate all secrecy or information bottlenecks."],"lenses":["4B.4","4B.6"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Attorney General Garland Announces Revised Justice Department News Media Policy","publisher":"U.S. Department of Justice","publication_date":"2022-10-26","url":"https://www.justice.gov/archives/opa/pr/attorney-general-garland-announces-revised-justice-department-news-media-policy","claim":"DOJ revised its media policy to restrict compulsory process against journalists except in limited circumstances and said the changes were meant to protect free and independent newsgathering.","locator":"lines 139-142","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary DOJ release on journalist protections and media-access rules.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 media-freedom policy.","ruler_attribution":"Biden administration DOJ policy choice.","contrary_evidence":["The policy is important but limited to DOJ process and does not end wider pressure on journalists."],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.4"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Press Freedom Index 2022 - Americas analysis","publisher":"Reporters Without Borders (RSF)","publication_date":"2022-05-03","url":"https://rsf.org/en/classement/2022/americas","claim":"RSF said the United States improved in the 2022 index partly because of Biden-administration changes such as reinstating regular White House and federal agency press briefings, while noting unresolved chronic press-freedom problems.","locator":"lines 43-44","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent press-freedom monitor with a specific 2022 country assessment and a clear attribution to the administration.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 media-freedom assessment.","ruler_attribution":"Biden administration credited for a limited improvement in press access.","contrary_evidence":["RSF explicitly says chronic issues remained unaddressed, so the improvement was partial."],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The Fight Isn’t Over","publisher":"Brennan Center for Justice","publication_date":"2022-01-20","url":"https://www.brennancenter.org/our-work/research-reports/fight-isnt-over","claim":"The Brennan Center said Biden would have signed the Freedom to Vote: John Lewis Act immediately if Congress had advanced it and warned that threats to democracy were real and growing.","locator":"lines 310-313","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent democracy-research organization offering a contemporaneous assessment of Biden’s pro-voting stance and the legislative blockage.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 voting-rights debate.","ruler_attribution":"Biden's stated willingness to sign reform; congressional blockage limited results.","contrary_evidence":["The source emphasizes Congress failed to act, so the result was constrained by legislative opposition."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.7","4B.8","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Democracy Wins the 2022 Midterms","publisher":"Brennan Center for Justice","publication_date":"2022-11-09","url":"https://www.brennancenter.org/our-work/analysis-opinion/democracy-wins-2022-midterms","claim":"The Brennan Center said the 2022 elections were free, fair, and calm, election deniers lost key races, and Congress advanced Electoral Count Act reform that would make a difference.","locator":"lines 198-215","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent election-governance analysis with concrete 2022 outcome claims and reform context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 election-period outcome and reform context.","ruler_attribution":"Partial Biden-era trajectory evidence, not a direct claim of personal orchestration.","contrary_evidence":["The piece also notes ongoing gerrymandering, money-in-politics problems, and pressure on democracy."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.2","4B.8","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The White House Can Do More to Expand Voting Access","publisher":"American Civil Liberties Union","publication_date":"2022-03-07","url":"https://www.aclu.org/news/voting-rights/the-white-house-can-do-more-to-expand-voting-access","claim":"The ACLU said Biden's voting-access executive order created an unprecedented opportunity to expand ballot access, but argued the White House and states needed to do more to implement it fully.","locator":"lines 20-34","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Independent civil-liberties critique that acknowledges progress while flagging incomplete implementation.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"2022 commentary on the administration's voting-access implementation.","ruler_attribution":"Biden administration executive action and implementation pressure.","contrary_evidence":["The source is supportive of the order but clear that implementation remained incomplete."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.7","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"One Year Later: How the Biden Administration is Doing on Civil Rights and Civil Liberties","publisher":"American Civil Liberties Union","publication_date":"2022-01-24","url":"https://www.aclu.org/news/capital-punishment/one-year-later-how-the-biden-administration-is-doing-on-civil-rights-and-civil-liberties","claim":"The ACLU said the Biden-Harris administration had made some progress on civil rights and civil liberties but that much remained to be done, including on speech, privacy, technology, and voting.","locator":"lines 11-23, 26-34","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Independent contemporaneous review that is useful as a mixed assessment of the administration's rights record.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"2022 midterm-period rights assessment.","ruler_attribution":"Biden administration generally, with only partial direct attribution.","contrary_evidence":["The article is a broad scorecard and does not isolate presidential responsibility for every issue area."],"lenses":["4B.4","4B.6","4B.7","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Remarks on Signing the Respect for Marriage Act","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2022-12-13","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/remarks-signing-the-respect-for-marriage-act","claim":"Biden signed the Respect for Marriage Act and said it protects marriage equality and interracial marriage in federal law, with people free to decide who to marry without government interference.","locator":"lines 77-80, 110-122, 132-134","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary presidential signing remarks tied to a concrete rights-expanding enactment in 2022.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 enactment and signing.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Biden signature and advocacy.","contrary_evidence":["The law is a major equality measure, but it does not by itself resolve broader anti-LGBTQ or anti-minority politics."],"lenses":["4B.7","4B.10"]}

**Ledger**
- Candidates found: about 40
- Documents opened: 13
- Duplicates collapsed: 8
- Irrelevant or out-of-period items discarded: 6
- Inaccessible or technical-error items: 3
- Accepted distinct URLs: 11
- Distinct domains in accepted set: 6

**Gaps**
- I did not reach the requested 30-open-document discovery depth before the web tool hit its call ceiling.
- The accepted set is still biased toward official U.S. sources plus a few strong independent monitors; it needs more Reuters/AP and more outside-U.S. democracy scholarship to reach the target domain mix.
- More direct evidence on 2022 media pressure, election administration at the state level, and any presidential use of federal leverage against opponents would sharpen 4B.2, 4B.5, and 4B.9.
- Next best queries: archived Reuters/AP coverage of the 2022 midterms and Biden’s democracy speeches, Congressional Record / House-Senate materials on the Electoral Count Reform Act, and additional independent media-freedom assessments that specifically discuss 2022 U.S. conditions.
